WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.675
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.234
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:15.820
Unit eleven: Migration Studies. Today you will explain the causes of migration with 'because of' and 'due to'.

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.633
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:22.927
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:24.935
Migration.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.331
Migration means the movement of people from one place to another.

8
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:32.195
Labour migration to Thailand is common.

9
00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:35.366
Your turn. Say: Migration.

10
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.317
Migration.

11
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:41.305
Diaspora.

12
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:45.698
Diaspora means people living outside their ancestral homeland.

13
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:49.130
The Cambodian diaspora in the USA is large.

14
00:00:49.717 --> 00:00:52.508
Your turn. Say: Diaspora.

15
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:56.612
Diaspora.

16
00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.479
Push factor.

17
00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:02.969
Push factor means a condition that drives people away from a place.

18
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:05.687
Drought is a powerful push factor.

19
00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:09.284
Your turn. Say: Push factor.

20
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.204
Push factor.

21
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.102
Pull factor.

22
00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:19.440
Pull factor means a condition that attracts people to a place.

23
00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.363
Higher wages are the main pull factor.

24
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.533
Your turn. Say: Pull factor.

25
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:29.554
Pull factor.

26
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:31.411
Assimilation.

27
00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:35.821
Assimilation means adopting the culture of the new society.

28
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:38.730
Assimilation can weaken the home language.

29
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:42.515
Your turn. Say: Assimilation.

30
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:46.600
Assimilation.

31
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.490
Integration.

32
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:54.060
Integration means taking part fully in a new society while keeping one's identity.

33
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.056
Language classes support integration.

34
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.059
Your turn. Say: Integration.

35
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.006
Integration.

36
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:05.763
Identity.

37
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.400
Identity means a person's sense of who they are.

38
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:13.028
Second-generation migrants may have a mixed identity.

39
00:02:13.617 --> 00:02:15.979
Your turn. Say: Identity.

40
00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:19.776
Identity.

41
00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:21.297
Border.

42
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:25.049
Border means the line separating two countries.

43
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.708
Thousands cross the border each day.

44
00:02:28.317 --> 00:02:30.272
Your turn. Say: Border.

45
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:33.867
Border.

46
00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:38.097
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.121
Visal, why do so many young Cambodians work in Thailand or South Korea?

48
00:02:44.217 --> 00:02:55.449
Mostly due to economic push factors: low farm incomes and debt. Because of higher wages abroad, families send one member to work and send money home.

49
00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.004
Are there other reasons?

50
00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:08.886
Owing to climate change, droughts and floods have made farming riskier. And social networks matter: people go where relatives already live.

51
00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:12.227
What happens to their identity abroad?

52
00:03:13.317 --> 00:03:24.862
It often becomes layered. Because of distance, migrants value Khmer traditions more, yet their children may feel at home in two cultures.

53
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.965
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.817 --> 00:03:31.838
Grammar focus: Reason clauses (because of, due to).

55
00:03:32.138 --> 00:03:33.185
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.883 --> 00:03:37.009
People migrate due to economic push factors.

57
00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:41.321
Families send one member abroad because of higher wages.

58
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:44.994
Farming has become riskier owing to climate change.

59
00:03:45.933 --> 00:03:54.382
'Because of / due to / owing to' + noun phrase; 'because / since / as' + clause. In formal writing 'due to' is common after 'be': 'The decline was due to drought.'

60
00:03:55.117 --> 00:03:58.733
Be careful! Don't say: They migrated due to they were poor.

61
00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:02.968
Say: They migrated because they were poor.

62
00:04:03.268 --> 00:04:06.138
'Due to' + noun; 'because' + clause.

63
00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:09.446
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.233 --> 00:04:11.525
Why People Move.

65
00:04:11.825 --> 00:04:17.481
Migration has always been part of human history, but its scale has grown dramatically in the modern era.

66
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:25.691
Researchers distinguish between push factors, such as poverty or conflict, and pull factors, such as employment or safety.

67
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:32.995
Due to improved transport and communication, migrants today maintain closer ties with their home communities.

68
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.353
Remittances sent by migrant workers now contribute significantly to many developing economies, including Cambodia's.

69
00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:47.325
Because of these connections, diasporas increasingly shape the culture and politics of their countries of origin.

70
00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:52.238
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:53.083 --> 00:04:54.816
They moved blank the floods.

72
00:04:55.116 --> 00:04:58.618
A, because. B, because of. C, due.

73
00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.178
The answer is B: because of.

74
00:05:05.317 --> 00:05:06.991
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:07.291 --> 00:05:13.986
A, He left due to he had no job.. B, He left due to unemployment.. C, He left due unemployment..

76
00:05:17.533 --> 00:05:20.845
The answer is B: He left due to unemployment..

77
00:05:21.983 --> 00:05:24.947
A condition that attracts people to a place is a blank.

78
00:05:25.247 --> 00:05:29.306
A, push factor. B, pull factor. C, border.

79
00:05:32.867 --> 00:05:34.947
The answer is B: pull factor.

80
00:05:36.083 --> 00:05:39.279
People living outside their ancestral homeland form a blank.

81
00:05:39.579 --> 00:05:43.430
A, diaspora. B, integration. C, identity.

82
00:05:46.967 --> 00:05:49.268
The answer is A: diaspora.

83
00:05:50.417 --> 00:05:54.309
According to the reading, remittances contribute significantly to…

84
00:05:54.609 --> 00:05:59.887
A, many developing economies. B, border control. C, assimilation only.

85
00:06:03.433 --> 00:06:06.524
The answer is A: many developing economies.

86
00:06:07.733 --> 00:06:10.885
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.876
What push or pull factor might cause someone to migrate?

88
00:06:31.683 --> 00:06:33.102
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.402 --> 00:07:11.369
A common push factor in rural Cambodia is the lack of stable income from farming, often made worse by debt and unpredictable weather. Due to these pressures, many young people feel they have no choice but to look for work elsewhere. At the same time, higher wages in Thailand or South Korea act as a strong pull factor. For instance, a month's salary abroad can equal several months' income at home. However, migration also has costs, such as separation from family, so the decision is rarely easy.

90
00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:13.459
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:19.220
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reason clauses (because of, due to).

92
00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.950
Today's value is empathy. Think about how others feel.

93
00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.902
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:07:26.202 --> 00:07:29.052
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

